// WHY THE IMAGE CARRIES NO OBJECT IDENTITIES AT ALL.
//
// insert_derived does NOT re-derive record.identity -- it takes the caller's word for it. Inside
// this repository that is sound, because store_node is the only producer and it derives the identity
// from the canonical node it just stored. A DECODER IS A SECOND PRODUCER, and one that reads
// identities from a file it does not control. Had the image carried identities, a decoder would hand
// insert_derived a record whose stated identity was a lie and nothing anywhere would refuse it: the
// store would answer find_object for a locator whose payload was never hashed to it, and checkout
// would reconstruct a program the commit never named. That is the forgeable-carrier class DESIGN
// section 4b describes, arriving through persistence rather than through the substrate.
//
// The image therefore records STRUCTURE ONLY -- kind, edge labels, and which earlier object each
// edge points at -- and every ObjectId is re-derived on load by replaying store_node. Forging an
// identity is then not caught, it is UNWRITABLE: the format has no field to lie in. This is DESIGN
// section 5's construction-over-validation applied to the one boundary where the store stops being
// the only author of its own contents.
//
// The same move dissolves the integrity check the first design carried. An earlier shape recorded
// the commit root's digest beside the objects so load could compare it against the re-derived root.
// With identities gone the root is an INDEX, its identity is derived like every other, and there is
// no claim left to check -- the comparison disappeared because nothing asserts anything, not because
// the check was skipped.

// EDGES POINT BACKWARDS BY POSITION, AND THAT IS WHAT PRESERVES SHARING.
//
// A nested-tree image would expand a shared subgraph once per reference, so a DAG whose sharing is
// the whole point of content addressing would serialize exponentially. Recording objects in
// dependency order and letting each edge name an EARLIER position keeps one entry per distinct
// object. A position cannot forge an identity the way a digest could, because the identity at that
// position is still derived from the bytes that follow it; a corrupted position names a different
// object or no object at all, and the second refuses.
// ONE ARM FOR MEMBER READS, NOT FOUR RESTATED ONES. gunbc.scm.json_member owns what reading a named
// member off a JSON object can fail with; this vocabulary owns what is wrong with a CLOSURE DOCUMENT
// specifically. Restating that population here put the same four facts under two authorities, and
// the consequence was measured rather than hypothetical: the repository envelope needed the same
// reader, imported these arms to get it, and so reported its OWN member failures as closure-document
// refusals -- a document whose closure was never read.
type ClosureDocRefusal
  = ClosureDocMemberReadRefused { cause: MemberReadRefusal }
  | ClosureDocFormatUnrecognized { found: String }
  | ClosureDocRootPositionUnresolved { reference: String }
  | ClosureDocUnexpectedMember { key: String }
  | ClosureDocTargetReferenceRefused { cause: TargetReferenceRefusal }
  | ClosureDocObjectTableRefused { cause: ObjectTableDecodeRefusal }

// THE ENCODE DOMAIN IS ITS OWN TYPE, and this split is a correctness fix rather than tidiness.
//
// ClosureDocIncompleteWithoutAdmission is produced by encode_complete_closure_document and by nothing
// else -- no decode path can reach it. It nevertheless sat in ClosureDocRefusal, which the LOAD
// classifier matches exhaustively, so closure_document_load_standing was forced to assign a standing
// to a state the loader cannot produce. It answered LoadDocumentMalformed, and malformed is the ONE
// standing that permits overwriting a newer document (gunbc.scm.supersession
// standing_permission_refusal). An encode-only state therefore had a route to "may supersede".
//
// That is a closed match over a dishonest domain: exhaustiveness is satisfied, the compiler is
// content, and the arm is answering for something that cannot occur. Splitting the type does not
// hide the arm -- it makes the question unaskable, because the classifier's parameter can no longer
// name this cause. DESIGN section 4b's top rung: not validated, not proven, unrepresentable.
type ClosureDocEncodeRefusal
  = ClosureDocIncompleteWithoutAdmission { identity: String }
  | ClosureDocRequiresNodeAtWrongKind {
      source_occupied: List<ObjectId>,
      manifest_occupied: List<ObjectId>
    }

// THE FORMAT TAG MEANS "I UNDERSTAND THIS EXACT SCHEMA", not "I understand some subset of whatever
// this writer may have meant". So once the tag matches, every JSON object in the document has a
// closed, variant-specific member set and anything else is a typed refusal.
//
// Silently ignoring an unknown member lets a writer state a fact the reader discards while still
// returning a successful load -- and re-encoding then deletes that fact. That is not forward
// compatibility, it is successful information loss, which is exactly the fabricated-plausible-output
// failure DESIGN section 5 forbids: the document loaded, so nothing anywhere reports that a claim in
// it was thrown away.
//
// It does foreclose additive evolution WITHIN a version, and that is the intended cost: a schema
// that grows a member grows a new format tag, and a decoder that supports both says so by admitting
// both tags. An open extension point, if one is ever genuinely needed, is a declared member with
// declared namespacing and retention rules -- never every unrecognized key promoted to one by
// default.
//
// The context is a closed coproduct rather than a String because the member sets differ BY VARIANT:
// an Atom kind carries `identity` and a Conj kind must not, so "unexpected here" is only meaningful
// relative to which shape was being read.
// ONE ARM, BECAUSE THE CLOSURE DOCUMENT HAS EXACTLY ONE MEMBER SET OF ITS OWN.
//
// This coproduct used to carry nine. The other eight described object-table entries -- a node entry,
// the kind variants, the edge variants, and after the manifest arrived an authored-source entry, a
// manifest entry and a manifest entry's own members -- and they moved to gunbc.scm.object_table_json
// because none of them is a fact about a closure document. The test that decided each was whether
// the context could exist in a repository object table with no closure document anywhere near it.
//
// The node-entry and kind and edge shapes went down too, and NOT because there are two table
// consumers now. They went because those are literally one schema in both tables, so keeping a copy
// here would give one schema two authorities -- the fork the move exists to close, not a symmetry
// worth preserving.
// AND WITH ONE MEMBER SET LEFT, THE CONTEXT FIELD GOES TOO. A discriminator that can hold exactly
// one value discriminates nothing; carrying it would be one fact written in two places, and every
// consumer would have to match an arm that cannot vary. The context returns the day a second closure
// member set exists, and not before.
fn member_set_refusal(v: JsonValue, allowed: List<String>) -> ClosureDocRefusal? {
  match unexpected_member_key(v: v, allowed: allowed) {
    Absent => none
    Present { value: key } => Present { value: ClosureDocUnexpectedMember { key: key } }
  }
}

type ClosureDocLoadOutcome
  = ClosureDocLoaded { store: ObjectStore, root: SemanticNodeTarget }
  | ClosureDocRefused { cause: ClosureDocRefusal }

// THE TAG MOVES BECAUSE THE SCHEMA GREW A MEMBER, WHICH IS THIS MODULE'S OWN STATED POLICY.
//
// The comment above says it in as many words: the format tag means "I understand this EXACT schema",
// additive evolution within a version is deliberately foreclosed, and a schema that grows a member
// grows a NEW FORMAT TAG. The object table now admits `{"source": ...}` entries, which a reader of
// the v2 schema refuses. Continuing to write "…-v2" over that would have been a meaning fork under
// DESIGN section 3 -- one name, two materially different contracts -- and it would have been THIS
// module's policy broken by this module. Named by external review of gunbc#9891.
//
// ONE TAG, NOT TWO, AND THE POPULATION WAS COUNTED RATHER THAN ASSUMED EMPTY. A decoder supporting
// both versions would say so by admitting both tags. The closure document has no durable instances:
// its only producer is a witness rebuilt from source every run. The REPOSITORY envelope beside it
// does have some -- checked-in fixtures under dag/test/fixture/scm_repository_load -- and those are
// versioned test inputs updated with the schema they exercise, not data whose old spelling anyone
// is owed. Neither is a live population that a compatibility arm would serve, and an arm with no
// consumer is machinery this repository refuses to carry.
// V4, AND THE VERSION MOVES BECAUSE THE LANGUAGE THIS FORMAT CAN EMIT GENUINELY CHANGED.
//
// v3 admitted two entry shapes, {kind, children} and {source}. Its writer serialized the whole
// store, so authored sources appeared in commit-closure documents -- but nothing could ever REACH
// one from a commit root, because every traversable edge from a SemanticNodeTarget is again a
// SemanticNodeTarget. Those entries were repository inventory arriving through a whole-store
// encoder, not content of the closure the document names.
//
// v4's writer derives the root-reachable population, so it can never emit a {source} entry, and its
// reader refuses one. That is a materially NARROWER language under the same protocol, which is
// exactly why the tag has to move: this module's own policy is that a format tag means "I understand
// this exact schema", and holding v3 while the emitted language shrinks would be the same false
// version claim as holding it while the language grows, run in the other direction. Under the
// one-current-version policy a v3 document is UnsupportedProtocol here -- refused as a predecessor
// rather than read as malformed.
//
// The repository object table keeps the mixed vocabulary and versions separately; sources belong to
// the repository image, which is the layer that always owned them.
data closure_document_format_tag: String = "gunbc-scm-commit-closure-v4"

// THE TABLE A COMMIT CLOSURE ACTUALLY HAS, WHICH IS NODES AND NOTHING ELSE.
//
// Every object here came back from derive_semantic_closure, so the parameter cannot name an authored
// source or a corpus manifest and there is no arm for either to be written honestly or dishonestly.
// That is what the mixed encode_object below stopped being able to promise once a third object kind
// existed: it has no refusal channel, so a manifest reaching it had no answer that was not a lie.
//
// ON THE DUPLICATED INDEX, declared rather than left to be discovered. EncodePositions carries both
// by_identity and nodes_by_identity, and over a nodes-only table those two maps are equal by
// construction -- one fact in two places, which is exactly what DESIGN section 2 prices. It survives
// here only because the type is still shared with the mixed repository table, where the two maps
// genuinely differ. It dissolves when the mixed node+source+manifest object-table codec is extracted
// to repository grain and this module's positions carry node positions alone.
// dissolve-on: the repository object-table codec owning its own object vocabulary and accumulator,
// at which point by_identity leaves this module with it.
fn encode_node_step(acc: EncodeAcc, record: ObjectRecord) -> EncodeAcc {
  let key = object_id_key(identity: record.identity.locator)
  let reference = position_reference(n: acc.positions.next)
  EncodeAcc {
    positions: EncodePositions {
      by_identity: map_insert(m: acc.positions.by_identity, key: key, value: reference),
      nodes_by_identity: map_insert(m: acc.positions.nodes_by_identity, key: key, value: reference),
      next: acc.positions.next + 1,
    },
    objects: concat([encode_record(positions: acc.positions, record: record)], acc.objects),
  }
}

fn encode_node_table(nodes: List<ObjectRecord>) -> EncodeAcc {
  match fold(
    nodes,
    init: EncodeAcc {
      positions: EncodePositions {
        by_identity: empty_map(),
        nodes_by_identity: empty_map(),
        next: 0,
      },
      objects: [],
    },
    f: fn(acc, record) { encode_node_step(acc: acc, record: record) }
  ) {
    EncodeAcc { positions: positions, objects: reversed } =>
      EncodeAcc { positions: positions, objects: reverse(reversed) }
  }
}

// THE ROOT IS AN ORDINARY REFERENCE, and it takes both arms for the same reason every edge does: a
// closure may legitimately be shipped without the object its own root names (a delta whose recipient
// already holds it). The predecessor encoded the root positionally too, so that case was as
// unnameable as any other.
fn encoded_root_reference(acc: EncodeAcc, root: ObjectId) -> JsonValue {
  encode_target(positions: acc.positions, target: root)
}

type ClosureDocEncodeOutcome
  = ClosureDocEncoded { value: JsonValue }
  | ClosureDocEncodeRefused { cause: ClosureDocEncodeRefusal }

// AN INCOMPLETE CLOSURE IS NOW REPRESENTABLE, SO THE QUESTION CHANGES FROM "CAN IT BE WRITTEN" TO
// "WAS IT DECIDED".
//
// The predecessor refused here, because a document with an unresolvable reference could not be
// loaded and writing it would have reported success for bytes that cannot be read -- a fabricated
// success. That refusal was right for a format that could not name what it lacked. This one can, so
// a partial closure is a legitimate document and its wire form is well-formed; whether a partial
// closure may be PUBLISHED is a policy question that does not belong in a codec (DESIGN section 3).
//
// What must NOT happen is the refusal quietly becoming optional -- a caller who forgets, and a
// partial document written by accident. The two questions are therefore two ENTRY POINTS rather than
// one entry point with an optional token:
//
//   encode_complete_closure_document(closure)      refuses if anything is uncontained
//   encode_admitted_partial_closure_document(a)    total; the admission IS the decision
//
// WHY THE PARTIAL ENTRY TAKES ONE ARGUMENT. Its predecessor took a closure PLUS an optional
// admission and checked only that the admission was PRESENT -- so an admission minted for closure A
// authorized encoding closure B, and no amount of checking inside this function could have noticed,
// because the token carried no subject. The admitted carrier holds its own closure, so there is no
// second closure to disagree with it and the mismatch has no spelling. That is why this entry
// performs no validation at all: there is nothing left to validate.
//
// RUNG, STATED HONESTLY BECAUSE THE CARRIER'S STRENGTH IS NOT THE PATH'S. The subject binding is
// structural. This GATE is still only mechanically preventable: encode_closure_document below
// remains callable directly, so a caller bypassing these entries can emit uncontained arms with no
// admission at all. The wall is on the carrier now, but the module's front door is still ajar.
// dissolve-on: module-private functions in .dag, at which point the unchecked encoder stops being
// reachable and the gate becomes structural. Until then this comment is the only thing saying so.
// THE COMPLETE WRITER CONSUMES THE WHOLE CENSUS, NOT THE ABSENT HALF.
//
// This asked unresolved_identities, which projects `absent`. For a requirement whose locator is
// occupied by an authored source the absent population is EMPTY, so this saw nothing missing and
// returned bytes -- the wrong-kind state was publishable through the front door while partial
// admission refused it at the side door. Kind is judged first because it is not a degree of
// incompleteness: no supply step repairs it.
fn encode_complete_closure_document(closure: CommitClosure) -> ClosureDocEncodeOutcome {
  match admit_well_kinded_closure(closure: closure) {
    NotWellKinded { source_occupied: sources, manifest_occupied: manifests } =>
      ClosureDocEncodeRefused {
        cause: ClosureDocRequiresNodeAtWrongKind {
          source_occupied: sources,
          manifest_occupied: manifests,
        }
      }
    WellKinded { admitted: image } =>
      match unresolved_identities(closure: closure) {
        missing =>
          if list_length(items: missing) == 0 {
            ClosureDocEncoded { value: encode_closure_document(image: image) }
          } else {
            ClosureDocEncodeRefused {
              cause: ClosureDocIncompleteWithoutAdmission {
                identity: an_uncontained_key(missing: missing)
              }
            }
          }
      }
  }
}

// TOTAL, AND THE TOTALITY IS THE CLAIM. Every way of obtaining an AdmittedPartialCommitClosure runs
// through a mint that derived the unresolved population from this very closure, so by the time one
// exists there is nothing this function could refuse that the mint did not already settle.
fn encode_admitted_partial_closure_document(admitted: AdmittedPartialCommitClosure) -> JsonValue {
  encode_closure_document(image: admitted_well_kinded(admitted: admitted))
}

// The first identity that would have been written as an uncontained arm. Naming one is what makes
// the refusal actionable; naming all of them is the caller's own query via unresolved_identities.
// AN uncontained key, not THE FIRST. Same correction as an_uncontained_target in repository_envelope:
// the refusal needs one actionable example and no consumer depends on which, so the name should not
// promise an ordering nothing verifies.
fn an_uncontained_key(missing: List<ObjectId>) -> String {
  fold(missing, init: "", f: fn(acc, id) {
    if acc == "" { object_id_key(identity: id) } else { acc }
  })
}

fn encode_closure_document(image: WellKindedClosure) -> JsonValue {
  let root = well_kinded_root(admitted: image)
  let acc = encode_node_table(nodes: well_kinded_nodes(admitted: image))
  let root_reference = encoded_root_reference(acc: acc, root: node_target_locator(target: root))
  json_object(
    members: [
      json_kv(key: "format", value: json_string(s: closure_document_format_tag)),
      json_kv(key: "objects", value: json_array(elements: acc.objects)),
      json_kv(key: "root", value: root_reference),
    ]
  )
}

// THE CLOSURE'S OWN TABLE DECODE, WHICH IS WHERE v4 REFUSES A SOURCE -- AND WHERE ITS POSITIONS STOP
// BEING ABLE TO HOLD ONE.
//
// The accumulator is NodeDecodeAcc, whose position map is Map<String, SemanticNodeObjectRef>. That
// type is what retired ClosureDocRootIsAuthoredSource and ClosureDocRootIsCorpusManifest: a contained
// root resolves against these positions, and the carrier has no constructor for a source or a
// manifest, so the wrong-kind refusals had nothing left to refuse. They were arms in a closed result
// type that no producer could produce -- the dishonest-domain class this module's encode/decode split
// already closed once -- and they are deleted rather than kept as defence, which would have forced
// closure_document_load_standing and every exhaustive consumer to answer for states outside the
// decoder's domain.
//
// The alternative of resolving the root against the decoded STORE was considered and refused: an
// ObjectStore is deliberately mixed, so it cannot prove the proposition, and it would turn an
// intrinsic document-local fact -- "the object at this position is my root" -- into a store-relative
// observation, blurring the one distinction the uncontained arm exists to keep.
//
// It is a separate step rather than a flag on the mixed one because the two formats have genuinely
// different closed member sets: a commit-closure entry is {kind, children} and nothing else, while a
// repository object-table entry is that OR {source} OR {manifest}. A shared step with a leniency
// switch would put the two vocabularies back into one authority and make the narrower one the one
// that has to remember to be strict.
//
// A {source} or {manifest} entry here refuses by its OWN name rather than as a missing `kind`. Both
// are well-formed entries of the repository table, so reporting them as malformed node entries would
// name the wrong defect -- the not-applicable-rendered-as-malformed failure this module's header
// already records, which is precisely what a caller pointing a v4 reader at a v3 document or a
// repository table would hit first.
fn decode_node_step(acc: NodeDecodeAcc, object_value: JsonValue) -> NodeDecodeAcc {
  match acc.refusal {
    Present { value: _ } => acc
    Absent =>
      match table_member_set_refusal(
        v: object_value,
        allowed: ["kind", "children"],
        context: ObjectTableSemanticNodeMembers
      ) {
        Present { value: set_cause } => node_decode_acc_refused(acc: acc, cause: set_cause)
        Absent => decode_node_entry_into_node_table(acc: acc, object_value: object_value)
      }
  }
}

fn decode_node_table(object_values: List<JsonValue>) -> NodeDecodeAcc {
  fold(
    object_values,
    init: NodeDecodeAcc { store: empty_store(), positions: empty_map(), next: 0, refusal: none },
    f: fn(acc, object_value) { decode_node_step(acc: acc, object_value: object_value) }
  )
}

// The format tag is checked BEFORE any object is decoded, so a file from a future format refuses as
// a format mismatch rather than as a pile of unknown-tag errors from the middle of a walk.
fn decode_closure_document(v: JsonValue) -> ClosureDocLoadOutcome {
  match read_string_member(v: v, key: "format") {
    MemberFailed { cause: c } => ClosureDocRefused { cause: ClosureDocMemberReadRefused { cause: c } }
    MemberString { value: format } =>
      if format != closure_document_format_tag {
        ClosureDocRefused { cause: ClosureDocFormatUnrecognized { found: format } }
      } else {
        match member_set_refusal(v: v, allowed: ["format", "objects", "root"]) {
          Present { value: c } => ClosureDocRefused { cause: c }
          Absent => decode_closure_document_body(v: v)
        }
      }
    _ => ClosureDocRefused { cause: ClosureDocMemberReadRefused { cause: MemberReadWrongShape { key: "format" } } }
  }
}

// THE WRAP IS THE LAYER BOUNDARY, AND IT IS ONE CONSTRUCTOR BECAUSE THE TABLE NOW SPEAKS FOR
// ITSELF. Every cause arriving from decode_node_table was raised by the object table about one of
// its entries, so the closure's only job here is to say where it came from -- not to have supplied
// the vocabulary the table refused in, which is what it used to do.
fn decode_closure_document_body(v: JsonValue) -> ClosureDocLoadOutcome {
  match read_member(v: v, key: "objects") {
    MemberFailed { cause: c } => ClosureDocRefused { cause: ClosureDocMemberReadRefused { cause: c } }
    MemberArray { elements: object_values } =>
      match decode_node_table(object_values: object_values) {
        NodeDecodeAcc { store: store, positions: positions, next: _, refusal: refusal } =>
          match refusal {
            Present { value: c } => ClosureDocRefused { cause: ClosureDocObjectTableRefused { cause: c } }
            Absent =>
              match read_member(v: v, key: "root") {
                MemberFailed { cause: c } => ClosureDocRefused { cause: ClosureDocMemberReadRefused { cause: c } }
                MemberObject { value: root_value } =>
                  match decode_target_reference(target_value: root_value) {
                    TargetReferenceRefused { cause: c } =>
                      ClosureDocRefused { cause: ClosureDocTargetReferenceRefused { cause: c } }
                    TargetUncontained { digest: root } =>
                      ClosureDocLoaded { store: store, root: SemanticNodeTarget { locator: root } }
                    TargetContainedAt { reference: reference } =>
                      match map_lookup(m: positions, key: reference) {
                        Present { value: root } =>
                          ClosureDocLoaded { store: store, root: node_target_of(reference: root) }
                        Absent =>
                          ClosureDocRefused { cause: ClosureDocRootPositionUnresolved { reference: reference } }
                      }
                  }
                _ => ClosureDocRefused { cause: ClosureDocMemberReadRefused { cause: MemberReadWrongShape { key: "root" } } }
              }
          }
      }
    _ => ClosureDocRefused { cause: ClosureDocMemberReadRefused { cause: MemberReadWrongShape { key: "objects" } } }
  }
}
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Publication standing
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// THIS FORMAT'S REFUSALS, CLASSIFIED BESIDE THE REFUSALS THEMSELVES.
//
// The standing vocabulary is format-agnostic and lives in gunbc.scm.load_standing; the mapping from
// THIS format's causes into it belongs HERE, next to the coproduct it reads, so a new refusal
// variant fails to compile where its author already is rather than in a consumer that has never
// heard of it. A consumer computing this by matching on ClosureDocRefusal would be reaching into one
// format's error enum for a format-agnostic answer -- the placement defect that closed gunbc#8940.
//
// WHY UNKNOWN MEMBERS AND UNKNOWN VARIANT TAGS ARE MALFORMED, NOT A NEWER WRITER, which follows from
// this module's own policy rather than from taste: a schema that grows a member grows a NEW FORMAT
// TAG. So a conforming newer writer emits a new tag; it does not emit this one carrying extras. A
// document under a MATCHED tag with an unrecognized member or variant is a broken document of THIS
// schema, and calling it "needs a compatible reader" would name a remedy that cannot arrive, because
// no conforming writer produced it.
//
// WHY AN UNRESOLVED POSITION IS MALFORMED AND NOT A FETCHABLE ABSENCE. A position denotes nothing
// outside the document that defines it, so an unresolved `at` names no object anyone could supply.
// Fetchable absence is the UNCONTAINED arm, which is not a refusal at all and never reaches here.
//
// WHY A COLLISION IS ITS OWN STANDING. object_store states both colliding records are legitimate and
// the forged pairing is prevented by construction, so a collision means the structural locator
// cannot represent both -- not that either is damaged. Classifying it as malformed would let a
// reader DISCARD A WRITER'S VALID GENERATION over an honest hash coincidence.
// THE MEMBER-READ ARM DESCENDS ON PURPOSE, and collapsing it to `cause: _` would be the failure
// mode that costs the most in this particular function. All four member-read causes really do share
// one remedy today, so the collapsed form is total, compiles, and looks complete -- and a NEW
// member-read mode would then inherit LoadDocumentMalformed in silence. Malformed is the SOLE
// standing that permits superseding a newer generation, so an inherited arm sits one step from a
// reader overwriting a writer's valid document -- one step and no longer the last one, since
// gunbc.scm.supersession additionally binds actuation to the generation the evidence was read from. Descending makes the next mode fail to compile
// here, where whoever adds it can see what it would cost.
// THE OBJECT-TABLE ARM BINDS ITS PAYLOAD AND PASSES IT ON; IT DOES NOT DISCARD IT. A bare binding in
// the arm carrying the payload is the tell DESIGN names for "total at the level examined, blind one
// level down", so it is worth saying which this is: the cause is handed to the authority that owns it
// and descends per cause, rather than collapsed into one standing here. Reading it as the defect
// would invert the fix -- classifying the six object-table causes at THIS grain is exactly what would
// hand an identity collision the one standing permitting supersession.
//
// The two remaining nested matches below are descent-in-place over populations whose owners carry no
// classifier (gunbc.scm.json_member, gunbc.scm.target_reference). They are not inconsistent with the
// delegating arm above: the object-table module owns a classifier and those two do not, so each cause
// is classified by whoever owns it.
//
// BOTH OF THOSE NESTED MATCHES DESCEND ALL THE WAY, which the target-reference one did not: its
// TargetReferenceMemberReadRefused arm bound the payload bare and answered one standing for the
// whole of MemberReadRefusal, while the sibling ClosureDocMemberReadRefused arm enumerated the same
// four causes. The two arms now agree, so a new member-read cause fails to compile at every position
// this function reaches it rather than only at the undecorated one.
fn closure_document_load_standing(cause: ClosureDocRefusal) -> LoadStanding {
  match cause {
    ClosureDocFormatUnrecognized { found: _ } => LoadUnsupportedProtocol
    ClosureDocMemberReadRefused { cause: c } =>
      match c {
        MemberReadSubjectNotObject => LoadDocumentMalformed
        MemberReadMissing { key: _ } => LoadDocumentMalformed
        MemberReadDuplicated { key: _ } => LoadDocumentMalformed
        MemberReadWrongShape { key: _ } => LoadDocumentMalformed
      }
    ClosureDocUnexpectedMember { key: _ } => LoadDocumentMalformed
    ClosureDocRootPositionUnresolved { reference: _ } => LoadDocumentMalformed
    ClosureDocObjectTableRefused { cause: c } => object_table_load_standing(cause: c)
    ClosureDocTargetReferenceRefused { cause: c } => target_reference_load_standing(cause: c)
  }
}

// A LOAD THAT SUCCEEDED STILL HAS A STANDING, and it is not always LoadComplete. This is the arm the
// predecessor format could not produce: a document whose uncontained references leave objects the
// store does not hold loads correctly and is PARTIAL, which is a success with an obligation rather
// than a failure. Completeness is derived from the objects, never a flag beside them.
// THREE ANSWERS, BECAUSE "NOT COMPLETE" IS TWO DIFFERENT FACTS. This asked closure_is_complete and
// answered LoadPartial for every false, which reported "fetch the named identities and this completes"
// for a closure whose requirement sits on a file -- unfetchable, because the locator is occupied. The
// census already separates the populations; this reads them all instead of collapsing them.
//
// IT READS THE WRONG-KIND POPULATIONS THROUGH wrong_kind_identities RATHER THAN NAMING ONE, which is
// the repair for a defect this same change introduced. When the census grew a THIRD population for a
// corpus manifest at a semantic requirement, this still asked only source_occupied -- so a manifest
// there answered LoadComplete or LoadPartial, telling a reader to fetch an object that is not
// missing. The compiler could not catch it: adding a field to a record breaks no match. Asking the
// census for its whole wrong-kind population means a fourth kind cannot reintroduce it either.
fn loaded_closure_standing(store: ObjectStore, root: SemanticNodeTarget) -> LoadStanding {
  let census = closure_census(closure: CommitClosure { store: store, root: root })
  if list_length(items: wrong_kind_identities(census: census)) > 0 {
    LoadRequiresNodeAtWrongKind
  } else {
    if list_length(items: census.absent) == 0 {
      LoadComplete
    } else {
      LoadPartial
    }
  }
}

fn closure_document_standing(outcome: ClosureDocLoadOutcome) -> LoadStanding {
  match outcome {
    ClosureDocLoaded { store: store, root: root } =>
      loaded_closure_standing(store: store, root: root)
    ClosureDocRefused { cause: cause } => closure_document_load_standing(cause: cause)
  }
}
